Test Scenarios: Anchor vs Supplemental Reviews
These are manual/scenario-level tests, distinct from the unit tests already in the suite.

Scenario 1 — Single anchor, no supplementals (anchor-only mode)
Setup: One version, one review (Solution Architect) with 5 risks, 2 decisions (1 open, 1 accepted), 1 open weakness.

Action: POST /run with selected_review_ids: [r1] only.

Expected:

anchor_only: true
consensus_points contains all 5 risks (every anchor finding is consensus)
divergent_points is empty []
open_decisions contains the 1 open decision
confirmed_decisions contains the 1 accepted decision
unresolved_weaknesses contains the 1 open weakness
Every item in consensus_points has exactly 1 entry in source_reviews with is_anchor: true
provenance_summary has exactly 1 entry
Specific checks for anchor role:

The single review's review_id appears as the anchor in provenance_summary[0].is_anchor = true
anchor_text on every consensus item equals text
supplemental_texts is [] on every item
Scenario 2 — Anchor + one supplemental, full overlap
Setup: Two reviews for the same version. Both raise identical findings (same wording) across all categories.

Action: POST /run with anchor_review_id: r1, selected_review_ids: [r1, r2].

Expected:

anchor_only: false
All items are in consensus_points; divergent_points is empty
Every consensus item has 2 entries in source_reviews — one with is_anchor: true, one with is_anchor: false
text on each consensus item matches the anchor's wording, not supplemental's
Specific anchor vs supplemental check:

anchor_text = text from r1 (anchor)
supplemental_texts[0] = text from r2 (may be identical or near-identical)
If both have the same text, supplemental_texts[0] == anchor_text is acceptable
Scenario 3 — Anchor + one supplemental, no overlap
Setup: Two reviews, zero shared findings. Anchor has risks A, B. Supplemental has risks C, D.

Action: POST /run with anchor_review_id: r1.

Expected:

consensus_points is empty []
divergent_points has 4 items (A, B, C, D)
A and B: present_in_reviews: [r1], absent_from_reviews: [r2]
C and D: present_in_reviews: [r2], absent_from_reviews: [r1]
A and B: anchor_text set, supplemental_texts: []
C and D: anchor_text: "", supplemental_texts: ["C"] / ["D"]
source_reviews on A/B has only the anchor prov; on C/D has only the supplemental prov
Key anchor behaviour check: Anchor's own unique findings are still tagged is_anchor: true in their source_reviews entry, even in divergent.

Scenario 4 — Anchor + one supplemental, partial overlap
Setup: Anchor has [X, Y, Z]. Supplemental has [X, W]. X is shared; Y, Z are anchor-only; W is supplemental-only.

Action: POST /run with anchor_review_id: r1.

Expected:

consensus_points: [X] — source_reviews has both reviews
divergent_points: [Y, Z, W]
Y: present_in_reviews: [r1], absent_from_reviews: [r2]
Z: same as Y
W: present_in_reviews: [r2], absent_from_reviews: [r1]
merged_findings: [X, Y, Z, W] — 4 unique items, no duplicates
X in merged_findings: source_reviews has both reviews
Scenario 5 — Three reviews, strict consensus definition
Setup: r1 (anchor), r2 (supplemental), r3 (supplemental). Item X is in r1 and r2 but NOT in r3.

Action: POST /run with selected_review_ids: [r1, r2, r3].

Expected:

Item X is in divergent_points, NOT consensus_points
present_in_reviews for X: [r1, r2]
absent_from_reviews for X: [r3]
This is the critical multi-supplemental test. Consensus requires presence in ALL selected reviews, not just a majority.

Scenario 6 — Anchor status authority over decisions
Setup: Same decision point "Cloud provider selection" appears in both anchor (r1, status: "accepted") and supplemental (r2, status: "open").

Action: POST /run with anchor_review_id: r1.

Expected:

Decision appears in confirmed_decisions, NOT open_decisions
status: "accepted" (anchor's status wins)
source_reviews contains both r1 (anchor) and r2
Why this matters: The anchor is the trusted reference. Supplemental opinions on decision status should not override it.

Scenario 7 — Anchor vs supplemental on weakness status
Setup: Same weakness "API gateway not rate-limited" in anchor (r1, status: "addressed") and supplemental (r2, status: "open").

Action: POST /run with anchor_review_id: r1.

Expected:

Weakness does NOT appear in unresolved_weaknesses (anchor says addressed)
The anchor's status: "addressed" takes precedence
Contrast: If the anchor had status: "open" and supplemental had status: "addressed", the weakness SHOULD appear in unresolved_weaknesses (anchor says open).

Scenario 8 — Switching the anchor between runs
Setup: Version has r1 (Solution Architect) and r2 (Delivery Manager). First run uses r1 as anchor.

Action 1: POST /run with anchor_review_id: r1, selected_review_ids: [r1, r2]. Note reconciliation_id = A.

Action 2: POST /run with anchor_review_id: r2, selected_review_ids: [r1, r2]. Note reconciliation_id = B.

Expected:

Run 1: r1 items canonical, r1 decision statuses authoritative
Run 2: r2 items canonical, r2 decision statuses authoritative; different output shape is expected if the two reviews have different text or status on shared items
GET /reconciliation after Run 2 returns B, not A
provenance_summary[0].is_anchor in Run 2 points to r2, not r1
This validates the anchor selection has real semantic effect, not just label.

Scenario 9 — Provenance detail on a consensus item
Setup: Anchor r1 has artifact_refs: [{artifact_id: "a1", artifact_name: "arch.docx", section_reference: "§3"}]. Supplemental r2 has no artifact refs.

Action: POST /run. Inspect a consensus item.

Expected:

source_reviews on the consensus item has 2 entries
Entry for r1: is_anchor: true, review_persona set
Entry for r2: is_anchor: false, review_persona set, artifact_id: "" (missing fields empty, not null, not error)
provenance_summary[0].artifact_refs for r1 contains the arch.docx entry
provenance_summary[1].artifact_refs for r2 is []
Note: _make_provenance (used for consensus items) does NOT carry artifact refs — that is only in provenance_summary. This is a known design simplification. Validate it is consistent and documented.

Scenario 10 — Re-selection and re-run
Setup: Version has r1, r2, r3.

Action 1: POST /select with anchor=r1, selected=[r1, r2]. Run reconciliation.

Action 2: POST /select with anchor=r1, selected=[r1, r2, r3]. Run reconciliation.

Expected:

GET /selection returns the second selection (3 reviews)
GET /reconciliation returns the second output (different reconciliation_id)
First output is gone — it was overwritten. This is intentional; verify user understands this from the UI (result card updates in place).
Risks Before Sprint 4
Ordered by impact × likelihood.

Risk 1 — Broken anchor-change affordance (UI)
What: The anchor pre-population hint says "You can change it below" but the current UI provides no mechanism to actually change the anchor. The anchor chip is static and hardcoded to the currently open review. Impact: Non-technical users follow the hint, cannot find the control, lose trust in the panel. Likelihood: High — any user who reads the hint will try. Owner: Must be resolved before Sprint 4 or the hint must be reworded to "Currently open review is your anchor" with no reference to changing it.

Risk 2 — merged_findings is not connected to the output sections in the UI
What: The result card renders consensus_points, divergent_points, open_decisions, confirmed_decisions, unresolved_weaknesses — but merged_findings is computed and stored and never shown. Sprint 4 proposal prep will need to consume it. Impact: If Sprint 4 reads merged_findings and finds unexpected shape (e.g., items only from anchor because anchor was processed first in _merge_all_findings), proposals will silently miss supplemental-only findings. Likelihood: Medium — depends on how Sprint 4 consumes the output. Mitigation: Confirm that merged_findings order (anchor-first) is acceptable for downstream consumers. Document the processing order explicitly before Sprint 4.

Risk 3 — Decision status conflict resolution may surprise users
What: When the anchor says a decision is "accepted" and a supplemental says "open", the anchor wins (by design). However, there is no visible indicator in the result card that a status conflict was resolved. The user sees the confirmed decision with no record that the supplemental disagreed. Impact: Users may incorrectly believe there is full agreement on a decision that is actually contested. Likelihood: Medium in real usage (different personas frequently disagree on decision readiness). Mitigation before Sprint 4: Either surface conflict metadata in the result card, or add a status_conflict: bool field to confirmed decision items so Sprint 4's proposal layer can flag them.

Risk 4 — Cross-project review ID validation only checks existence, not version membership
What: save_reconciliation_selection validates that each review ID exists on the project (store.get_review(rid) is None), but does NOT validate that the review belongs to the specified version_id. A user could pass review IDs from a different version of the same project. Impact: Reconciliation runs across versions silently — the output will contain findings from version A mixed with version B. Likelihood: Low in current single-user flow, higher once multi-user or API-only usage grows. Mitigation before Sprint 4: Add a review.version_id == version_id check in save_reconciliation_selection or run_reconciliation.

Risk 5 — Reconciliation output is overwritten on re-run with no history
What: SQLite uses INSERT OR REPLACE — previous output is permanently gone after a re-run. There is no audit trail of past reconciliations. Impact: If a user runs reconciliation, shares the reconciliation_id with a colleague, then re-runs, the colleague's reference is broken. More importantly, Sprint 4's proposal layer will reference reconciliation_id from the output — if reconciliation is re-run after a proposal draft is created, the proposal will be based on a stale reconciliation that no longer exists. Likelihood: Medium — any iterative workflow triggers this. Mitigation before Sprint 4: At minimum, store reconciliation_id on the proposal record so it can be validated. Consider whether re-run should produce a new row or update in place. Decide and document the policy before Sprint 4 builds on top of it.

Risk 6 — _token_overlap is sensitive to word order and punctuation
What: "No DR strategy defined" and "DR strategy not defined" have 3/5 tokens in common = 0.60 Jaccard — below threshold. They would be treated as distinct and both appear in the output. The 0.70 threshold is conservative by design, but real review text is noisy — reviewers write the same concern differently. Impact: Consensus under-reported; divergence over-reported. Users conclude reviews disagree more than they do. Downstream proposal might be unnecessarily cautious. Likelihood: High in practice — review text comes from LLM persona outputs which use varied phrasing. Mitigation before Sprint 4: This is an acknowledged design tradeoff. The risk is: Sprint 4 builds proposal language on divergent findings that are actually the same concern. Flag this for the Sprint 4 implementer. Do not change the threshold without a data sample to calibrate against.

Risk 7 — Checkbox state is read from DOM at submit time only (no intermediate validation)
What: onRunReconciliation reads checkboxes at click time. If a user opens the panel, checks supplemental reviews, navigates away (causing a re-render), then returns — the checkboxes may be in a default state (unchecked), silently dropping the user's prior selection. Impact: User believes they are reconciling 3 reviews but only the anchor is submitted. Result is anchor-only with no warning. Likelihood: Low currently (single-drawer SPA), higher if drawer re-rendering is ever triggered mid-session. Mitigation before Sprint 4: Low priority now, but note that Sprint 4 should not assume the UI preserved the user's checkbox state across re-renders.

Risk 8 — RECONCILABLE_CATEGORIES constant defined but not enforced
What: models/reconciliation.py defines RECONCILABLE_CATEGORIES = ("risks", "assumptions", ...) but the service iterates all keys in review.findings regardless of that constant. A review with a custom category like "custom_risk_tier_2" will be reconciled silently. Impact: Minor now. Becomes a risk in Sprint 4 if the proposal template expects exactly the named categories and receives unexpected keys. Mitigation: Either enforce the constant in the engine or remove it from the model to avoid false documentation. Decide before Sprint 4 consumes the output shape.

